1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,080
 Alright, let's have a chat about our first advanced component in fluid power systems,

2
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:07,080
 the piston cylinder assembly.

3
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:11,400
 This is also the first transformer element for fluid power systems we're going to meet.

4
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:15,280
 Piston cylinder assemblies you've probably seen out in the real world, maybe even close

5
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:20,120
 up kind of heavy machinery, bobcat, bulldozers, whatever, whatever you need to kind of lift

6
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:21,120
 something heavy.

7
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:25,480
 There's usually a piston cylinder there to transfer hydraulic pressure into a force to

8
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:27,040
 do that.

9
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:31,640
 Using equipment, for example, airplanes and kind of spacecraft to kind of move the control

10
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:34,160
 surfaces around as well.

11
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:38,920
 These things work by a pretty simple principle that if you put a kind of force on one side

12
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:42,640
 of a piston cylinder on the plunger, that you'll get a pressure out on the fluid on the

13
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:47,120
 other side, right, as that piston has to kind of disperse that force over the fluid that

14
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:49,000
 acts on it, and vice versa.

15
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,920
 If you have a pressure kind of in a cylinder acting on a piston, well that's going to produce

16
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:56,480
 a force on that piston to maybe move a mass.

17
00:00:56,480 --> 00:01:00,720
 And this gives rise to the first CCR for kind of piston cylinder assemblies, that the force

18
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:04,800
 you get on a plunger is equal to the kind of pressure in the fluid times the area of

19
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:05,800
 the piston.

20
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:09,120
 There are different types of these depending on the different pistons you're dealing

21
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:10,120
 with.

22
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:14,600
 If you kind of have regular kind of piston cylinders, then it's kind of force times area.

23
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:17,840
 If you are fluid on both sides, pressure on both sides, well then you need to kind of take

24
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:21,240
 a difference, right, because you're going to have pressure kind of acting down on one

25
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:22,840
 and up on the other.

26
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:26,680
 You might have circumstances where the kind of area of the piston on the top is different

27
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:30,160
 to the area of the piston on the bottom because of the plunger.

28
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:33,320
 All of these circumstances are kind of covered in the textbook and the lecture summary

29
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:37,360
 for this week, and there's a slightly different CCR for all of them, but the kind of fundamental

30
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:41,560
 principle that you're turning a pressure into a force is the same amongst all of these.

31
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:45,960
 There's a kind of second CCR, a flow CCR for this transformer as well, and it has to do

32
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:48,240
 with the amount of volume that you displace.

33
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:52,720
 You can imagine that if a piston is to move a distance, say delta x, then the amount

34
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:55,800
 of volume that it's going to trace out, or the fluid that it's going to displace is going

35
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:59,520
 to be its kind of face area multiplied by that delta x.

36
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:02,640
 You can of course differentiate this if you want to to kind of get a relationship between

37
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:07,960
 an input velocity and a flow rate, but this flow CCR is basically saying for a given distance

38
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:09,520
 you trace out this much volume.

39
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:13,680
 I think the difficult thing about piston cylinder assemblies is the SSR, and that's that

40
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:19,040
 making sure that the way you define kind of your CCRs agrees with the way that you draw any

41
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:21,720
 net forces or pressures that are acting.

42
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:26,000
 For example, pressure inside of a cylinder should always be acting kind of, they call it

43
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,240
 hydrostatically, right, pushing outwards on all of the surfaces that it's touching.

44
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:33,480
 So if you have pressure in a cylinder acting on a piston wall and the force on that

45
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:38,000
 piston should be such that it's being pushed away from the fluid.

46
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,920
 In terms of flow, the SSR is that if your coordinate is kind of pointed positive say

47
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:47,440
 over to the right, then that piston as it transfers over to the right should create a flow

48
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:49,720
 that goes right.

49
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:51,480
 And those flow rates need to be defined accordingly.

50
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:55,040
 If you wanted to find the flow going kind of the opposite way to what it should according

51
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:59,080
 to the coordinate, then what you need to do is make sure that in the CCR you include

52
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:01,200
 a negative sign to account for that.

53
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:04,080
 Like many others in this course, I think this is a concept that's best explained through

54
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:05,080
 a problem.

55
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:06,600
 So let's have a go at problem 5.4 together.
